  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/af/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_96.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Don Blake finds himself in trouble with Jane Foster when he returns after an hour \"locked in his private lab.\"&nbsp; All his patients have left by now, and Jane is disgusted he doesn't seem to show any responsibility toward them.&nbsp; But what she doesn't know is that he was out as Thor, helping to rescue a bus that had plunged into the water.&nbsp; Thanks to his assistance, all its passengers were saved.\n\nThey hear news on the radio about the rescue, but also about the stone crypt of Merlin, the \"famed wizard of King Arthur's court,\" and both wonder if the legendary Merlin ever used his magic for good or evil.&nbsp; Dr. Blake doesn't know, and he states that it's \"no concern of <i>ours</i>!\"\n\nThe next morning, the crypt is unloaded for display at the museum, but when they open it to examine the remains, they find a perfectly preserved body, as if he had used magic to keep himself intact for the last thousand years.&nbsp; None of the scholars believe in magic and conclude there must be some scientific explanation for the body that looks only as if it were asleep -- not the dusty remains they were expecting.\n\nOnce the scientists leave to ponder this, Merlin rises, because of <i>course</i> he never died!&nbsp; We learn that he possessed telepathy, levitation and transportation powers that he used to impress the knights of the round table.&nbsp; He only made it <i>look</i> like it was magic, but all along, it was his own power -- the power of a mutant!\n\nReading a newspaper, he learns about JFK, the current \"king\" (which they call \"President\"), and intends to \"become the real power behind the country's leader\" just like he did in King Arthur's day.&nbsp; He decides to cause a rocket heading into earth orbit to go off course, intending to reveal that his power is something to be feared by the new government.&nbsp; But news of the rocket \"malfunction\" soon makes the news, where it is seen by Dr. Blake, who decides to become Thor in order to set it right before the ground crew is forced to destroy it to prevent casualties.\n\nAfter ensuring the rocket makes it into space, Thor initially suspects Loki as the cause, but when he arrives in Asgard to check, he finds Loki still bound as before, but in the process, Loki informs him of the <i>true</i> culprit -- that of Merlin, who is <i>not</i> dead as originally believed.&nbsp; To Thor's astonishment, he finds Loki as good as his word --\nMerlin's body has vanished from the crypt!\n\nMerlin decides he needs to rid himself of this meddling \"Thor\" person and heads to the White House, but when he sees JFK, he decides, \"He looks too young!&nbsp; I'll have to search further till I find the President!\"&nbsp; But by now, Thor has finally tracked him down, and the two come face-to-face in Washington DC.\n\nMerlin causes Thor's feet to stop working, rooting him to the spot and then levitates the entire Washington Monument to use as a javelin against him.&nbsp; Thor could smash it, but \"I dare not atomize a national shrine!\"&nbsp; Instead he hooks his hammer to the tip and redirects it back to where it belongs.\n\nUndaunted, Merlin then levitates the Pentagon and causes it to crash down on Thor, who creates a trench with his hammer to dive into just in time.&nbsp; \"Now to hack my way out!\"&nbsp; Merlin then animates the seated Abraham Lincoln statue from the Lincoln Memorial, but Thor is able to force him back into his seat.\n\nThor finally decides to gamble and changes back to Don Blake, telling Merlin he has the power to change into anything, and Merlin <i>believes</i> him and surrenders!&nbsp; Thor then commands him to return to the crypt for another thousand years.&nbsp; He has the word of Thor promising that he will remain safe, and he will be waiting if he ever awakens to threaten mankind again!&nbsp; Merlin complies, and he returns to the crypt, which is sealed \"forever.\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "A hollow Merlin tale where the <i>ancient wizard</i> proves astonishingly uninteresting.\nThe issue delivers nothing memorable -- just page‑count filler in mythic clothing.",
  "subtitle": "The Tale That Makes You Think You've Slept a Thousand Years",
  "review_text": "The story begins by consuming three pages that involve Thor rescuing a bus, while at the same time Dr. Blake ducks into his private lab even though his office is full of patients.&nbsp; This part of the story seems to be present only to allow Jane Foster to make an appearance and once again berate the doctor for the lack of responsibility he repeatedly demonstrates (unlike Thor).\n[ASSET: center | BusRescue | width=67%]\n\nBut by page 5, it really was time to kick off this month's story, which involved \"Mad Merlin,\" presumably the same Merlin who lived during King Arthur's day, and who managed to survive his entombment for the last thousand years inside a crypt, which he willingly entered, apparently to avoid his enemies, who mistakenly thought him dead.&nbsp; The spell he used revived him immediately upon contact with air when the crypt was again opened.\n\n[ASSET: left | MerlinTheMutant | width=26%] When Merlin revives, he indicates on page 7 that his true powers were always natural, as a result of being a \"mutant\" and that he only used \"sorcerer's hocus-pocus\" to make it <i>appear</i> like magic to his contemporaries.\n[BR]\n[BR] This is all well and good, but note that this is the third time this month the word \"mutant\" is used (see also [LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1|2026-11-16]).&nbsp; It simply <i>can't</i> be an accident that the debut issue of X-Men ([LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1|2026-11-25]) was published this same <i>week</i>.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Stan is so proud of his new explanation for enhanced abilities that he's using it every chance he gets!)]\n\nBut... Merlin also says on page 6 that he used a spell to keep his body alive for 1000 years.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=So which is it?&nbsp; You can't have it both ways!]\n\nWith his mutant powers of levitation, telepathy and teleportation, Merlin heads to Cape d'Or (near Nova Scotia) and causes a rocket to go off course.&nbsp; Thor sets it right, and in the process, is alerted to the <i>true</i> menace by the [SPAN: severity=info; text=very last person you'd expect to help -- Loki himself!]\n\n<i>Of course</i> Loki is monitoring things on Earth, and I liked the fact that he is still bound in uru chains where we last saw him in these pages.\n[ASSET: center | LokiTellsTheTruth | width=67%]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | MerlinMeetsThor | width=12%] Another eye-opening moment was when Merlin finally gets a glimpse of the President (JFK) and decides that he's simply <i>too young</i> to be anyone important (a <i>wonderfully</i> absurd conclusion that elicited a momentary chuckle on my part), but by the end of page 9, it was time for the final confrontation with Thor (which you just <i>knew</i> was going to be over in the course of the final four pages).\n\nAfter that, the story was nothing more than a series of obstacles for Thor to avoid (and I note that Thor finds he can't fly out of the way of a lowering Pentagon building on page 11 but somehow still has time to build a trench <i>under</i> it to hide in.&nbsp; Why not use that same time to fly free -- [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=you dope!])&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Also, Thor seems to have no qualms about blasting through the Pentagon floor!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | UnderThePentagon | width=13%] I suppose there's no sense in wondering how Thor was able to smash his way through the floor of the Pentagon building that had been lowered on him in <i>this</i> issue when in [LINK:1963:JIM_91_thor0|JIM #91], it took the Belt of Strength brought to him by two Valkyries after the evil Sandu lowered what appears to have been a building far <i>smaller</i> than the Pentagon over him.&nbsp; (I mean, <i>really</i>... compare this panel to the cover of that issue from five months ago -- the resemblance is uncanny.)\n\nBut the final insult was the quick way that Thor outwits Merlin in the end, changing to the mortal Don Blake and pretending that his \"magic\" allows him to transform into any shape he wants.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=And Merlin <i>buys it</i>... and immediately <i>surrenders</i>.]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]\n[ASSET: center | MerlinSurrenders | width=67%]\n\nThe [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved], not because of Thor's superior abilities, but rather (it <i>seems</i>) because the story ran out of pages!\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis was a forgettable tale with a villain that didn't even have enough intrigue to fill up as many as the 13 pages devoted to the feature.&nbsp; Four pages of filler at the beginning (counting the splash page) delays the villain's entrance, and then his meaningless search for the President on pages 8 and 9 allowed yet one more page to be consumed.\n\nEven after Thor arrived and the battle commenced at the top of page 10, it lasted for only three pages and consisted of nothing other than Thor warding off three hurled threats before the final, [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=and <i>absolutely unconvincing</i> resolution] drove Merlin back into his crypt.\n\nTo call this story \"middling\" would be <i>generous</i>.&nbsp; Merlin was so boring as a character, it defies belief that he ever got so much as an entire issue to menace the Thunder God.&nbsp; (In contrast, at least Sandu from [LINK:1963:JIM_91_thor0|JIM #91] was being manipulated by Loki, so there was a little more intrigue there, which is why it rated slightly higher.)\n\nWhy is it that all magicians must be evil?&nbsp; The Merlin we met in this tale was petty and bent immediately upon awakening on taking control (albeit behind the scenes).&nbsp; He also seems to latch on to the fact that he is a \"mutant\" very naturally, even though at this point, the Marvel readers are just now being exposed to the concept for the first time.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Even readers in the 1960s could <i>not</i> have missed the convenience of this explanation!]\n\nWere it not for the fact that I liked the brief interaction with Loki (who actually played a small, but meaningful role in the story) and got a momentary laugh at Merlin's conclusion that JFK was \"too young\" to be anyone important, this story wouldn't have rated as high as it did.&nbsp; But those two moments were the only ones trapped inside of this otherwise poor outing.\n\n<b>Finally</b>:\n[BR] I sure hope Thor was able to move the Pentagon back to its original spot\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(the story <i>never</i> addresses it -- not even in passing)].",
  "side_note": [
    "[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=A small nit:]\n[BR] Merlin's robes consist of stars, circles, crescent moons and ringed planets (a la Saturn).&nbsp; However, since the telescope was not invented until <i>many </i>centuries after King Arthur's time, it's unlikely that Merlin (or anyone else) would have known of the existence of a <b>ringed world</b> since they would never have seen one.\n\nIt's admittedly a <i>small</i> inconsistency that is easily missed on the part of the artist, but I still noted it."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "This version of Merlin is later known as \"Warlock,\" whom we meet in X-Men 30 (XMEN #30), perhaps as a result of Barry Smith's letter published in XMEN #28, where he reminds Marvel of the fact that Cerebro has never detected the presence of \"one of the oldest mutants that the world has spawned.\"\n\nThe response he receives indicates that Marvel had completely forgotten about Merlin.\n[BR] <i>So, we'll just put our heads together one of these days and see if we can't whip up a plot in which he -- but why tell you now?&nbsp; Just wait'n see, pal -- just wait'n see!</i>",
  "highlight": "Thor vs. Merlin delivered one momentary chuckle, but the \"wizard\" mostly put the issue to sleep -- and I nearly joined it."
}